+## The headline result
 
-- `GET /health/live` - process liveness.
-- CLI: `dotnet run --project src/Lab.Cli -- --help`.
+| Experiment | Changed factor | Result |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Q1 index** | covering index absent → present | **45.5 ms → 1.3 ms** (~34×); logical reads 35,218 → 6 |
+| **Q2 projection** | entity materialization → SQL projection | **2.5 ms → 1.4 ms** (~1.8×) |
+| **Q3 cache** | `Cache:Enabled` false → true | **p95 2.5 ms → 4.2 ms — the cache made it ~1.7× *slower*** |
 
-The application refuses to start outside Development/Testing (local-lab guard).
+Q3 is the one worth reading. The cache lost in all five repetitions, in both orders, with
+non-overlapping p95 ranges. That part is solid: each arm was a fresh API process, warmed by its
+own separate k6 process, measured for one constant-arrival-rate run.
+
+**The published `0.286` hit rate was the wrong window.** It was read from the process-wide counters
+*once*, after the measured run. Those counters are cumulative and the warmup drives traffic through
+the same process, so the number covered warmup and measurement added together — not the sixty
+seconds the latency came from. The orchestrator now snapshots the counters after the warmup exits
+and again after the measured run, and records the difference.
+
+Re-measured that way, the hit rate of the measured window is **0.167**, not 0.286 — and it
+reconciles exactly: cache lookups equal measured GETs in all ten runs, which is the check a single
+cumulative read can never pass. So roughly **83% of measured lookups miss**, and a miss costs a
+Redis round-trip *plus* the SQL read it was meant to avoid. In front of an indexed single-row
+lookup that already takes ~2 ms, there is very little left for a cache to save.
+
+Why so few hits: 1,000 fixture SKUs at 20/s over 60 s is ~1.2 requests per SKU, so most requests
+are the only request for that SKU in the window — and the warmup's entries age past the 60 s TTL
+partway through it. There is simply not much reuse for the cache to exploit here.
+
+The [re-run](results/published/20260913/q3-cache/) reproduced the latency finding as well, and more
+cleanly than the original: the cache lost in **all five repetitions on both median and p95**, with
+no overlap between the arms (cache-off p95 topped out at 5.10 ms; cache-on never went below
+7.50 ms). It was measured from a dirty tree — the changes that added the snapshots — with the diff
+published beside it.
+
+> It took three attempts. The second was thrown out because Redis became unreachable 23 times
+> mid-run: 133 dropped iterations, one request stalling for 103 seconds. The harness marked it
+> invalid and failed the whole comparison rather than averaging it in — which is the behaviour the
+> harness exists for, so [it is published too](results/published/20260913/q3-cache-attempt-redis-stall/).
+
+An unalternated, un-repeated, status-code-only load test against a warm cache would have
+reported the opposite.
+
